A||N||8||Dan87GT||window tint removal||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||04-10-2000||10:06 AM||||Does anybody have any good techniques for removing window tint? I have it on my back window and am afraid of removing it because I have a rear defroster and do no want to damage the elements. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||04-10-2000||10:43 AM||||I heard that if you soak it then cover it with a plastic trash bag for a bit it will peel off. Then you need to wash of the glue rather than scraping it||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-10-2000||10:50 AM||||Also remember to peel from top to bottom and then wash side to side. This is the best way to do it so you can minimize stress on the defroster lines. Oh one more thing go very very slow when peeling the tint off. If you see it staring to lift the defroster line soak it somemore and peel from the other direction. You may end up with some cracked lines but PepBoys makes a kit to fix the cracks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||04-10-2000||05:44 PM||||when i removed my window tint...i did it in the winter causse it just peeled right off the window....and then i just took some heavy duty window cleaner (lots of amonia) and wiped off any glue remaining.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-10-2000||07:52 PM||||Myself I don't like the stuff. it's rarely applied well and always looks like crap after a few years.<P>Most of the time I look at the stuff it's not real well stuck down along the heater lines. Part of that's because the line is raised and part is because I don't think the stuff really sticks well to anything but glass or something else really hard flat and non pourus. <P>If it does want to stick to the heater. Try hitting the rear defoger switch and let it run a minute before pealing the film. Start near a corner so you can control what you are doing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gokart||04-10-2000||08:45 PM||||Previous owner of my GT did a crappy job with the tint. Left gaps around 3rd brake light and didn't get it all the way across. A buddy of mine told me one of the only ways to get it off is with GASOLINE! He's nuts, I'm not going to have to smell gas for the next 6 months. I'm going to take a scraper to it. Don't really see the need of the defrost anyway with all the heat that comes up from the engine. I think it'll look better without BOTH the tint and defrost lines.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||04-10-2000||09:16 PM||||It is next to impossible to remove the tint without destroying the rear window defroster grid. The film itself is fairly easy to remove, especially if it was not applied well. A razor blade can be used to get it started coming off and just start peeling it. Once the film is removed, a solution of straight amonia and water soaked onto the glue will loosen it up pretty good. There is also a product made specifically for tint glue removal- check with a detail shop to see where they get it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rcn491||04-10-2000||09:21 PM||||I used a hair dryer to heat up the film at a edge, then I pulled slowly moving the dryer.<BR>The remaining glue can be taken off with a razor blade and /or lacquer thinner.||||reg||1
Z||000008||wkayl||04-10-2000||10:03 PM||||As I was scrolling down through the suggestions I was about to enter an idea.  Then I got to rcn491's suggestion and it was the same one that I was going to list.||||reg||1
